e-logging-monitoring walkthrough log — 2026-10-10
Worked-case lesson: no command line by design (brief §6).
The "run" is the event taxonomy + retention schedule + SOC workflow design authored
and self-reviewed in-session against NIST SP 800-92 (log-management lifecycle:
generation, transmission, storage, analysis, disposal).

Scope: fictional Beacon Logistics (120 staff, regional freight; continued from
e-bcp-drp). Scenario: program design for the BIA-critical dispatch system —
current state is local-only logs, "until disk fills" retention, untuned SIEM.
Artifacts authored (all fictional, labeled):
  - Event taxonomy: 6 categories (authentication, authorization, system/service,
    network security, application, physical/environmental) each mapped to a
    detection; 3 claimed detections with no source recorded as gaps.
  - Retention schedule: per-category hot/warm/cold tiers with written
    justifications (auth 90d/1y/7y; dispatch transactions 90d/1y/3y;
    firewall/IDS 30d/1y/—; DNS 30d/6m/—; debug 14d/—/—); disposal procedure +
    litigation-hold suspension rule.
  - Integrity & time: internal NTP pair, >60s skew alert, TLS forwarding to
    append-only collector, read/admin separation of duties.
  - SOC workflow: 2 analysts business hours + contracted MDR nights/weekends
    (15-min SLA), 5 Tier-1 playbooks, quarterly tuning review per alert with
    FP-rate measurement and retire-or-retune decisions, monthly coverage metric.

